 leverage the testing effect — retrieval IS the learning event.","papers":[{"title":"The Power of Testing Memory: Basic Research and Implications for Educational Practice","authors":["Roediger HL","Karpicke JD"],"year":2006,"evidenceLevel":"review","relevanceScore":98,"keyFindings":["Testing is a powerful learning event, not just assessment","Direct and indirect benefits of testing on memory","Practical implications for self-directed learning"]},{"title":"Test-enhanced learning: Taking memory tests improves long-term retention","authors":["Roediger HL","Karpicke JD"],"year":2006,"evidenceLevel":"rct","relevanceScore":98,"keyFindings":["Taking tests produces greater retention than restudying","Testing effect robust across multiple retention intervals","Free recall tests most effective for long-term memory"]},{"title":"Repeated retrieval during learning is the key to long-term retention","authors":["Karpicke JD","Roediger HL"],"year":2007,"evidenceLevel":"rct","relevanceScore":97,"keyFindings":["Repeated retrieval is more effective than repeated study","Dropping items from further study after retrieval does not impair retention","Retrieval practice is the key driver of durable memory"]},{"title":"Retrieval practice produces more learning than elaborative studying with concept mapping","authors":["Karpicke JD","Blunt JR"],"year":2011,"evidenceLevel":"rct","relevanceScore":95,"keyFindings":["Retrieval practice outperforms concept mapping for retention","Active retrieval more effective than elaborative encoding","Results challenge primacy of elaboration in learning"]},{"title":"Retrieval practice enhances new learning: The forward effect of testing","authors":["Pastötter B","Bäuml KHT"],"year":2014,"evidenceLevel":"review","relevanceScore":92,"keyFindings":["Prior retrieval practice enhances encoding of new material","Forward testing effect reduces proactive interference","Testing benefits extend beyond tested material"]},{"title":"Retrieval Practice in Classroom Settings: A Review of Applied Research","authors":["Adesope OO","Trevisan DA"],"year":2017,"evidenceLevel":"systematic_review","relevanceScore":90,"keyFindings":["Retrieval practice effective in real classroom settings","Benefits transfer to applied educational contexts","Effect sizes moderate to large in field studies"]},{"title":"Investigating the testing effect: Retrieval as a characteristic of effective study strategies","authors":["Smith MA","Blunt JR"],"year":2016,"evidenceLevel":"rct","relevanceScore":90,"keyFindings":["Retrieval is the active ingredient in effective study strategies","Strategies that incorporate retrieval outperform those that do not","Testing effect confirmed across multiple experimental conditions"]},{"title":"Optimized schedules of retrieval practice for durable and efficient learning","authors":["Rawson KA","Dunlosky J"],"year":2011,"evidenceLevel":"rct","relevanceScore":92,"keyFindings":["Criterion-based retrieval schedules optimize learning efficiency","Three correct retrievals per item is near-optimal","Schedules can be individualized for maximum benefit"]},{"title":"Effects of retrieval practice on retention and application of complex educational concepts","authors":["Corral D"],"year":2025,"evidenceLevel":"rct","relevanceScore":88,"keyFindings":["Retrieval practice benefits extend to complex concepts","Application and transfer improved by retrieval practice","Effects persist over extended retention intervals"]}]},{"id":"study-techniques","name":"Evidence-Based Study Techniques","icon":"📚","description":"Research identifies which study strategies actually work — and which popular ones don't.","practiceTip":"Practice testing and distributed practice are the two most effective techniques identified by research.","papers":[{"title":"Improving students learning with effective learning techniques","authors":["Dunlosky J","Rawson KA"],"year":2013,"evidenceLevel":"systematic_review","relevanceScore":98,"keyFindings":["10 learning techniques evaluated for effectiveness","Practice testing and distributed practice most effective","Comprehensive evidence-based framework for learning"]},{"title":"Strengthening the student toolbox: Study strategies to boost learning","authors":["Dunlosky J"],"year":2013,"evidenceLevel":"review","relevanceScore":96,"keyFindings":["Practice testing and distributed practice rated as highly effective","Highlighting and rereading rated as low utility","Evidence-based study strategies identified and ranked"]},{"title":"Making things hard on yourself, but in a good way: Creating desirable difficulties to enhance learning","authors":["Bjork EL","Bjork RA"],"year":2011,"evidenceLevel":"review","relevanceScore":95,"keyFindings":["Desirable difficulties enhance long-term learning and retention","Interleaving, spacing, and testing are key desirable difficulties","Short-term performance decrements mask long-term benefits"]},{"title":"Memory and metamemory considerations in the training of human beings","authors":["Bjork RA"],"year":1994,"evidenceLevel":"foundational_theory","relevanceScore":88,"keyFindings":["Introduced the desirable difficulties framework","Conditions that slow learning can enhance long-term retention","Storage strength vs retrieval strength distinction clarifies training design"]},{"title":"Elaborative interrogation: Generating why questions to enhance learning","authors":["Pressley M","McDaniel MA"],"year":1987,"evidenceLevel":"rct","relevanceScore":92,"keyFindings":["Asking why questions enhances fact learning","Elaborative interrogation improves memory more than reading","Generating explanations deepens encoding"]},{"title":"Dual coding theory and education","authors":["Paivio A"],"year":2006,"evidenceLevel":"review","relevanceScore":92,"keyFindings":["Dual coding (verbal + visual) enhances memory","Imagery and language systems provide complementary encoding","Educational applications widely validated"]},{"title":"The generation effect: A meta-analytic review","authors":[